I like the general idea here, BUT:

> * Half resistance is intrinsic resistance given by level gain or by
> eating stuff.
> * Full resistance is everything else (including intrinsics given at
> level 1.)

Is it possible up upgrade half resistance into full, maybe by getting
it from two sources?  I would think this would have to be possible.
Nethack+ had a similar system, where the first acquisition of a
resistance by eating would be temporary, and to truly gain it for good,
it had to be acquired again before the original source expired.  It is
worth noting that that part of Nethack+ did *not* make it into the
final game, probably for good reason.

I think it'd be nice to have the skinny on what having
"half-resistance" means in game terms.


> Also, "magic resistance" has been divided into two separate
> resistances, outlined below.

This is potentially a huge change....


> DIVISION OF MAGIC AND SPELL RESISTANCE:
>
> * Magic resistance now only protects against magic missiles and magical
> explosions.
> * Spell resistance now only protects against enemy spells (and, ahem,
> death rays - but I might change this in the future).

This makes magic resistance a lesser resistance, and spell resistance
becomes what magic resistance was.

I base this statement on the fact that, in vanilla Nethack, while magic
resistance is useful for many things, the thing that makes it necessary
to any non-challenge ascension kit, instead of just greatly
recommended, is that it protects against death rays, touch of death and
Death's actual touch.  These things can happen to a player with only
one turn of warning (since many of the monsters who can ToD can
teleport at will), and the Wizard himself can kill with a touch, so
going after the Book without magic resistance is dangerous with no
remedy other than magic resistance (or hallucination, but for good
protection on the ascent you'd have to be hallucinating practically the
whole time).

The problem with magic resistance in vanilla, I say, is that it's
simultaneously too essential to a winning game and available from too
few possible sources.  This makes this problem *worse*: using GDSM to
avoid touch of death no longer works, and you can't use quest artifacts
for it either!

> Because resistances given at level 1 are full, and resistances given
> above level 1 are half, the following changes have had to occur:
> * Barbarians now gain half poison resistance at level 3.

Why?  What's wrong with Barbarians having "full" poison resistance?
Again, what does this mean?

> BONUS FEATURES:
> Element damage has been standardised:
> * All fire damage will reduce your max HP by a small percentage of the
> damage recieved.

Does half fire resistance protect against max HP loss?  Remember that
Genhennom is full of fire traps, which is why fire resistance is
recommended before going there.


> * Half disintegration resistance will cause disintegration attacks to
> only halve your HP.

What about the wide-angle disintegration beam?

Overall it's an interesting concept, but I'd like to know a little more
about the details.

